<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora: the hybrid app framework for .NET + React, portable across Windows, Android and iOS — reference it plus ONE shell package. Three layers: Core is the contract (the typed IPC stack with its middleware dispatcher and scoped router, the event bus, the webview interception and file-serving pipeline, and the platform-neutral shell contracts — the IUiDispatcher UI-thread seam plus file dialogs, clipboard, URL launching, UI blocking and holding the window at an orientation, so application logic compiles with no Windows reference). Engine is the brain (the mission scheduler with claims, lanes and durable queues; journalled safe file mutation with path locks). Modules bridge .NET to the web (media probing, planning, remuxing, streamed range delivery and platform codec conversion; native file dialogs; Live Activities and playback sessions; request tracking; staged self-update with safe archive extraction; versioned resource packs whose boot decision cannot skip the packaged-vs-fetched version comparison; the system back gesture offered to the page per press, foreground transitions carrying how long the app was away, and holding the window portrait or landscape — which a page cannot do for itself, since its own screen.orientation.lock() works only in fullscreen). The outbound notification path reports what it accepted, filtered, dropped and delivered, so "my page receives no events" is answerable without guessing. Application host and builder via ShenoraApplication.CreateBuilder. Depends on Microsoft.Extensions DI (implementation) + logging abstractions.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The DI IMPLEMENTATION (not just Abstractions) is required: the builder calls
         BuildServiceProvider, which lives there. D17. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ⚠ ONE package, ONE type, and it is the narrower of two bad trades rather than a precedent.
      `generic-library.md` says a helper two packages need should be PUBLIC, because a ProjectReference
      does not grant internal access — that is why WinFormsUiDispatcher is public. `ActivityWire` is the
      exception the same rule allows: it is serializer OPTIONS, no adopter has asked for it (D15), and
      making it public would put a mutable JsonSerializerOptions on the SemVer surface forever to save
      one line here. The alternative that was actually in place — a copy in Shenora.iOS and another in
      the test — is what let the shipped payload and the golden payload diverge silently.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Shenora.iOS" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
